.TH SDGSRV 8
.SH NAME
sdgsrv \- serve a directory as 9P over Noise-secured datagrams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sdgsrv
[
.B -v
] [
.BI -c " ncache"
] [
.BI -d " n"
]
.I keydir
.I addr
.I dir
.br
.B sdgsrv -g
.I keydir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sdgsrv
is the responder of
.IR sdg (2),
as the styxbrick's firmware is, for an InferNode machine: it announces
the UDP address
.I addr
and serves
.I dir
to the one commander whose public key it holds, each accepted handshake
a session with an export of
.I dir
of its own.
The protocol is styxbrick's
.BR docs/STYX-DGRAM.md :
Noise IKpsk2 over X25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305 and SHA-256.
.PP
.I Keydir
holds, as lines of hex,
.B sdgsrv.key
(its X25519 private key),
.B sdgsrv.peer
(the commander's public key) and
.B sdg.psk
(the pre-shared key);
.B -g
makes a set, with the commander's side beside them
.RB ( sdg.key ,
.BR sdg.brick ,
.BR sdg.psk ,
as
.IR sdg (2)
and
.IR brick (1)
name them) to be copied to the commander.
It also keeps there, in
.B sdgsrv.ctr.a
and
.BR .b ,
the newest initiation counter it has accepted, so that an initiation
recorded from the wire is as old after a restart as before it; with a
store it cannot read or write it does not start, or does not answer.
.PP
.B -c
is the number of replies a session keeps to answer a request sent again
(16, as the brick keeps);
.B -d
loses one data packet in
.I n
each way, as a lossy link would, for tests;
.B -v
says when a session is made.
.SS "A flood of initiations"
An initiation costs two X25519s before anything shows who sent it, and
anyone holding the server's public key can make one that will be looked
at.
Four a second are looked at as they come.
Past that the server is under load: an initiation is answered with a
cookie, an HMAC of its sender's address under a secret that changes
every two minutes, sealed to the initiation, and no X25519 is done for
it, unless it carries the proof
.RB ( mac2 )
that its sender has had a cookie, which only a sender at that address
can have.
Senders that have are looked at, four a second each and sixteen a
second in all; at most 64 cookies a second are sent, each smaller than
the initiation it answers.
.IR Sdg (2)'s
client shows its cookie at once.
.SS "What it does not do"
A session is not rekeyed: the protocol has no rekey, so a session's
keys last until the commander makes the session again (a mount made
again is a new handshake, with new keys and a new export).
A session that has sent or received 2^60 packets is over.
There is no deferred promotion (STYX-DGRAM Rev S1): the brick has one
session, which a new one replaces only when a packet arrives under its
keys; here each handshake is a session beside the others, and only the
commander's key with a newer counter makes one.
The suite is Noise's, not CNSA's.
An idle session is dropped after two minutes.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sdgsrv.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR brick (1),
.IR sdg (2)
